  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "F001",
      "claim": "Allegretto, Dube, and Reich (2011) and Dube, Lester, and Reich (2010) argue that traditional state-level panel data studies fail to control for regional heterogeneity and pre-existing trends in low-skill labor markets.",
      "source_url": "https://whatweknow.inequality.cornell.edu/topics/does-raising-the-minimum-wage-negatively-impact-employment",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F002",
      "claim": "Allegretto, Dube, and Reich (2011) and Dube, Lester, and Reich (2010) contend that studies not using geographically proximate comparison groups produce spurious negative employment effects.",
      "source_url": "https://whatweknow.inequality.cornell.edu/topics/does-raising-the-minimum-wage-negatively-impact-employment",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F003",
      "claim": "Neumark, Salas, and Wascher (2014) argue that the border-county approach does not account for shocks correlated with minimum wage changes.",
      "source_url": "https://whatweknow.inequality.cornell.edu/topics/does-raising-the-minimum-wage-negatively-impact-employment",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F004",
      "claim": "Neumark, Salas, and Wascher (2014) argue that the evidence remains consistent with disemployment effects.",
      "source_url": "https://whatweknow.inequality.cornell.edu/topics/does-raising-the-minimum-wage-negatively-impact-employment",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F005",
      "claim": "Allegretto, Dube, and Reich (2011) estimated teen employment effects between 0.5% and zero.",
      "source_url": "https://irle.berkeley.edu/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/The-New-Wave-of-Local-Minimum-Wage-Policies-1.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F006",
      "claim": "Allegretto, Dube, Reich, and Zipperer (2017) reported an own-wage elasticity estimate of 0.043 with a standard error of 0.188.",
      "source_url": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w23584/revisions/w23584.rev0.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F007",
      "claim": "Dube, Lester, and Reich (2016) reported an own-wage elasticity estimate of -0.266 with a standard error of 0.383 for teens.",
      "source_url": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w23584/revisions/w23584.rev0.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F008",
      "claim": "Allegretto, Dube, and Reich argue that Neumark and Wascher (2008) failed parallel pre-trends tests.",
      "source_url": "https://irle.berkeley.edu/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/The-New-Wave-of-Local-Minimum-Wage-Policies-1.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F009",
      "claim": "Allegretto, Dube, Reich, and Zipperer conclude that using event studies, synthetic controls, or contiguous county controls results in small or zero estimated employment effects of minimum wage increases.",
      "source_url": "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6960e4e656321405090869aa/impacts_of_minimum_wages_review_of_the_international_evidence_Arindrajit_Dube_web.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F010",
      "claim": "Dube et al. and Allegretto et al. argue that studies failing to restrict comparison areas to geographically proximate regions produce biased results.",
      "source_url": "https://whatweknow.inequality.cornell.edu/topics/does-raising-the-minimum-wage-negatively-impact-employment",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "id": "F011",
      "claim": "Some analyses are criticized for producing employment loss estimates that are outliers compared to the broader body of research.",
      "source_url": "https://www.epi.org/publication/the-high-road-seattle-labor-market-and-the-effects-of-the-minimum-wage-increase-data-limitations-and-methodological-problems-bias-new-analysis-of-seattles-minimum-wage-incr",
      "confidence": "low"
    },
    {
      "id": "F012",
      "claim": "Some studies estimating employment elasticity fail to provide evidence that the policies under study changed wages.",
      "source_url": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w32925/w32925.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F013",
      "claim": "Time series analysis was the predominant method for estimating minimum wage employment elasticities until the early 1990s.",
      "source_url": "https://www.rfberlin.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/24025.pdf",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "id": "F014",
      "claim": "Modern methods for estimating minimum wage effects include synthetic control, border discontinuity designs, and stacked event-study approaches.",
      "source_url": "https://www.rfberlin.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/24025.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F015",
      "claim": "Minimum wage employment estimates may not apply to the majority of the workforce not employed in minimum-wage jobs.",
      "source_url": "https://poverty.ucdavis.edu/sites/main/files/file-attachments/invitedcommentary_leigh.pdf",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "id": "F016",
      "claim": "Studies may fail to include state unemployment rates as a covariate to account for non-minimum-wage factors affecting unemployment.",
      "source_url": "https://poverty.ucdavis.edu/sites/main/files/file-attachments/invitedcommentary_leigh.pdf",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "id": "F017",
      "claim": "There is disagreement over whether the range of existing employment elasticity estimates is due to methodological differences or publication bias.",
      "source_url": "https://econbrowser.com/archives/2015/03/23316",
      "confidence": "low"
    },
    {
      "id": "F018",
      "claim": "Card and Krueger (1995) found that researchers, reviewers, and editors may favor studies reporting a statistically significant negative employment effect.",
      "source_url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/ts-min-wage.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F019",
      "claim": "Researchers have discretion over control variables, functional forms, and sample selection to generate negative and significant employment effects.",
      "source_url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/ts-min-wage.pdf",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "id": "F020",
      "claim": "Doucouliagos and Stanley (2009) analyzed 64 U.S. minimum wage studies.",
      "source_url": "https://ideas.repec.org/a/bla/brjirl/v47y2009i2p406-428.html",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F021",
      "claim": "Doucouliagos and Stanley (2009) concluded that the literature does not support the existence of an adverse employment effect after correcting for publication selection bias.",
      "source_url": "https://ideas.repec.org/a/bla/brjirl/v47y2009i2p406-428.html",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F022",
      "claim": "Existing meta-analyses have examined U.S. time-series studies, international studies, and UK-specific studies.",
      "source_url": "https://www.hendrix.edu/uploadedFiles/Departments_and_Programs/Business_and_Economics/AMAES/UkMinWage%20final%20edits.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F023",
      "claim": "Neumark and Shirley (2021) found that 79.3% of studies reported negative employment effects following minimum wage increases.",
      "source_url": "https://aier.org/article/the-economics-of-the-minimum-wage-myths-facts-and-consequences",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F024",
      "claim": "Boockmann (2010) found that 67% of estimations in a meta-sample of 55 empirical studies showed negative signs.",
      "source_url": "https://mpra.ub.uni-muenchen.de/61321/1/MPRA_paper_61321.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F025",
      "claim": "Belman and Wolfson (2014) reported the largest reliable employment elasticities at -0.07.",
      "source_url": "https://mpra.ub.uni-muenchen.de/61321/1/MPRA_paper_61321.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F026",
      "claim": "Belman and Wolfson (2014) reported the smallest reliable employment elasticities at -0.04 for youth.",
      "source_url": "https://mpra.ub.uni-muenchen.de/61321/1/MPRA_paper_61321.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F027",
      "claim": "Belman and Wolfson (2014) reported the smallest reliable employment elasticities at -0.01 for the food and drinking sector.",
      "source_url": "https://mpra.ub.uni-muenchen.de/61321/1/MPRA_paper_61321.pdf",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F028",
      "claim": "Some meta-analyses suggest that averaging across studies points to an employment effect estimate near zero.",
      "source_url": "https://wol.iza.org/articles/employment-effects-of-minimum-wages/long",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "id": "F029",
      "claim": "Neumark and Shirley (2021) report that negative employment impacts are more pronounced among teens and young adults.",
      "source_url": "https://aier.org/article/the-economics-of-the-minimum-wage-myths-facts-and-consequences",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F030",
      "claim": "Neumark (2018) finds evidence of job losses among the least-skilled workers.",
      "source_url": "https://aier.org/article/the-economics-of-the-minimum-wage-myths-facts-and-consequences",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "F031",
      "claim": "Evidence suggests larger job reductions in the tradable manufacturing sector following minimum wage increases.",
